## 1. Introduction
The oxygen evolution reaction (OER) is an essential half reaction involved in a wide range of electrocatalytic processes, such as metal-air batteries, electrolytic water splitting, electrochemical reduction of ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and so on [1-3]. The OER is a kinetically sluggish reaction which involves a transfer of four electrons, and thus requires high overpotentials [4]. Currently, ruthenium and iridium based compounds are the popular benchmark electrocatalysts for the OER, offering low overpotentials [5]. These precious metal based electrocatalysts, however, have several detrimental drawbacks such as extremely low earth-abundance, high cost, and poor durability under large current density operations, which are intolerable for large scale applications [5]. Consequently, it is critically important to develop highly efficient and stable non-noble metal based electrocatalysts to achieve low overpotential OER operations, particularly under high current densities. In this connection, a wide variety of transition-metal based alloys [6, 7], metal oxides [8, 9], hydroxides [10, 11], sulphides [12, 13], selenides [14, 15], tellurides [16], phosphides [17-19], nitrides [20], and carbides [21] have been developed as electrocatalysts for the OER. Except for very few reports [22-25], the previously reported non-noble metal based OER electrocatalysts were evaluated their efficiencies and durability at low current densities such as less than $100\mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which are not suitable for commercial applications. For industrial applicability, the OER electrocatalysts should possess mechanical robustness and sustainable durability, and deliver high current densities (more than $500\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}$ ) at low overpotentials. In this regard, we have developed a few electrocatalysts to meet the above requirements, including $\mathrm{NiO/a-Fe_{2}O_{3}}@\mathrm{NF}$ composites [26], mixed NiO/NiCo2O4 nanocrystals [27], N-doped $\mathrm{C/Ni_{5}P_{4}/F e_{3}P}$ hollow nanocubes [28], and $\mathrm{NiFe}/(\mathrm{Ni},\mathrm{Fe})_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ core/shell nanowire arrays [29]. Nevertheless, there are still ample room for improvements through boosting the quantity and quality of exposed active sites of the catalysts with novel nanostructuring and composition design [1].
Presently, most of the non-precious metal based electrocatalysts are produced in powdery form and thus require binders to load them onto a holding substrate (current collector) such as glassy carbon [14], graphite plate [28], and so on, to fabricate the working electrode. Most of the binder materials, such as Nafion [14] and polyvinylidene difluoride [28], however, are poor electrical conductors. The use of binders would not only reduce the number of exposed active sites of the catalyst, but also decrease the overall electrical conductivity of the product electrode [30]. In addition, the contact and adhesion between the binder and the electrocatalyst may not be strong enough to stand against the large stresses created during the high current density operations because of the intense generation of product bubbles [31]. One of the effective strategies to tackle the above problem is in situ growth of electrocatalysts on the skeleton surfaces of conductive 3D macroporous substrates, such as nickel foam (NF) [30, 32].  In situ growth of the electrocatalysts on the skeleton surfaces of NF offers firm and close contact between the electrocatalysts and the substrate so that the charge transport across the electrocatalyst/substrate interfaces and mechanical stability of the electrodes can be much enhanced [30, 32].
Recently, multi-metal based electrocatalysts were developed to offer high degrees of freedom in composition design and thus to further boost the synergetic effect between constituent components for enhancements in electrochemical performances [33-38]. Multi-metallic components not only alter the lattice and bonding characteristics but also optimize the adsorption energies of the catalysts to achieve much improved electrocatalytic efficiencies, which may overcome the volcano limitation [39]. For instance, tri-metal based electrocatalysts, such as NiFeMo alloys [35], Ni5Co3Mo–OH nanosheets [36], FeCoNi phosphides [40], NiFeMo alloy inverse-opals [41], and NiFeCr layered double hydroxides [42], have recently been reported as high-efficiency OER electrocatalysts.
On the other hand, metal-organic framework (MOF) materials are a class of structurally welldefined nano-porous materials [43], exhibiting robust catalytic properties [44], high surface areas and pore volumes [45], fascinating structural diversity [46], and tailorable pore structure [47], and have recently been applied as electrocatalysts for the OER [48-52]. Because of their low electrical conductivity and low mass permeability characteristics, MOFs have rarely been used as direct OER electrocatalysts [50]. They are often utilized as precursors to derive carbon based composite catalysts, containing metals or metallic derivatives as active species, through high temperature thermal treatments [53-55]. The thermal treatment, however, would potentially damage the advantageous ordered porous structure of MOFs, leading to aggregation of metal centers, loss of active sites, and thus decay in efficiencies and durability [54-56]. In this regard, loading of catalytic MOFs on porous conductive substrates, such as in situ growth of MOFs on NF, is regarded as one effective way to overcome the low electrical conductivity and low mass permeability problems [48, 56-59]. Recently, we developed in situ grown MOFs on NF to form MOF/NF composite catalysts, which showed excellent electrocatalytic efficiencies along with outstanding durability at high current densities [57-59]. The MOF/NF system as direct electrocatalysts is proved to induce synergistic effects for enhancements in OER performances [48, 56-59]. In addition to the synergistic effect between the MOF and the conductive substrate, intra-molecular synergistic effects arising from interactions between constituent metal clusters of a bimetallic MOF, play a critical role to improve the OER performances [48, 56-59].
With the above considerations combined, the intra-molecular synergistic effects are further explored for trimetallic MOFs based on a maximized-entropy approach for enhancements in electrocatalytic performances. The basic concept of the maximized-entropy approach is to maximize the randomness of the distribution of the constituent active species of a catalyst to gain maximum synergistic effects among the constituent active species to achieve maximum enhancements in electrocatalytic performances. To induce synergistic effects, the participating active species should be in intimate contact. A way to ensure the maximum extent of interactions between participating active species is to maximize the randomness of the species distribution, which can be achieved by maximizing the molar configuration entropy of the system. For a multi-component system, the molar configurational entropy $(S_{\mathrm{config}})$ , taking only the active species into account, can be calculated by the following equation:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{S_{\mathrm{config}}=-R\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}l n(x_{i}),}\end{array}
$$
where $R$ is the universal gas constant, $N$ is the number of active species, and $x_{\mathrm{i}}$ is the molar fraction of active specie $i$ . Evidently, the molar configurational entropy is a function of $x_{i}$ and can be shown to possess a maximum by requiring $\partial S_{\mathrm{config}}/\partial x_{i}=0$ and $\partial^{2}S_{\mathrm{config}}/\partial x_{i}^{2}<0$ subject to the constrain $\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}=1$ . The maximum can be derived to occur at ${x}_{i}=1/N$ , i.e., the active species are equimolar in the catalyst. It is to be cautioned that the above derivation applies only to catalysts composed of molecularly well-mixed constituents, and it only ensures a maximum extent of interactions between constituents. For catalysts composed of constituents with micro-domains or grain boundaries, not molecularly well-mixed, the above derivation is not applicable. Also, positive intra-molecular synergistic effects between constituents are a prerequisite for the catalyst design discussed here.
Based on the above conclusion derived from the maximized-entropy concept, equimolar Fe, Co, Ni based trimetallic MOFs were grown in situ on the skeleton surfaces of NF as OER electrocatalysts. The equimolar Fe, Co, Ni based trimetallic MOF, using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ (1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid) as the organic linker, was successfully grown in situ on the skeleton surfaces of NF with a simple one-step solvothermal process as illustrated in Scheme 1. The product MOF appears as thin nanoslabs, firmly anchored on the skeleton surfaces of NF, to form a binder-free $[\mathrm{FeCoNi(OH)_{3}(B D C)_{1.5}}]/\mathrm{NF}$ , denoted as FCN-MOF/NF, working electrode for the OER. The FCN-MOF/NF electrode delivered OER current densities of 10, 100, 500, and $1,000\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ at extremely low overpotentials of 196, 225, 260, and 284 mV, respectively along with a remarkably small Tafel slope of $29.5~\mathrm{~mV~dec^{-1}}$ . It outperforms corresponding non-composition-balanced trimetallic, bimetallic, and monometallic MOF/NF electrodes, and is superior to the benchmark OER electrode, $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}/\mathrm{NF}$ , and most of the top tier OER electrocatalysts reported in recent years. More importantly, the FCN-MOF/NF electrode exhibited excellent stability under operations at an extremely large current density of $1,000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , making it a strong candidate for commercial alkaline electrolytic water splitting.
## 2. Experimental Section
## 2.1. Chemicals and Characterizations
Iron(II) chloride tetrahydrate $\mathrm{(FeCl_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O,98\%)}$ , cobalt(II) chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(CoCl_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ , $98\%$ ) and nickel(II) chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $98\%$ ) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. 1,4- Benzenedicarboxylic acid (H2BDC, $98\%$ ) was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Potassium hydroxide (KOH, $85\ \%$ ) was purchased from Honeywell International Inc. Iridium(IV) oxide $(\mathrm{IrO}_{2},99\%)$ ) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. Deionized water (DI water, electrical resistance ${\sim}18~\mathrm{M}\Omega$ ) was produced with a Milli– $\mathbf{\cdotQ}\mathbf{\circled{R}}$ Advantage A10 Water Purification System. All chemicals and reagents were of analytical grade and used without further purification. Commercial nickel foam (NF) (thickness: $1.8\mathrm{mm}$ ) was purchased from Changsha Liyuan Port (Changsha, China) and cut into pieces of $2.7~\mathrm{cm}\times2.7~\mathrm{cm}$ , for the in situ growth of MOF on NF under solvothermal conditions. To remove the thin oxide layer present on the skeleton surfaces of NF, NF was ultrasonicated in 3M HCl for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , followed by washing with DI water, ethanol, and acetone, and drying in a vacuum oven at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for an hour. The morphology of the MOFs grown on NF was characterized with a field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM) (Hitachi S-4800, Japan). Energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX or EDS) spectroscopy (Oxford 6857, Oxford Instruments) was conducted with an accelerating voltage of $15\mathrm{kV}$ to determine the elemental composition of the catalyst. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (JEOL 205 3000F, JEOL Ltd.) was conducted to study the morphology and composition of the MOF removed from the corresponding MOF/NF electrode. The crystalline structure of the MOF samples was examined with a powder X-ray diffractometer (Shimadzu XRD-6000, Japan) equipped with a 3 kW Cu X-ray tube. Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were recorded on a PerkinElmer spectrometer (Spectrum Two) using the KBr pellet technique. Inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) was conducted to quantify metallic elements present in the MOFs. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted to study the chemical state of the elements with a monochromatized Al Kα X-ray as the excitation source (XPS, Thermo ESCALAB 250XI, USA).
## 2.2. Fabrication of Electrodes
The trimetallic MOF, FCN-MOF, was grown on the skeleton surfaces of NF by following the reported procedures for the synthesis of Fe and Co based bimetallic $[(\mathrm{Co_{1-X}F e_{X}})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})]$ -MOF with proper modifications [60]. Here, F is for Fe, C for Co, and N for Ni in the notation of FCN-MOF. Briefly, $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{\bfBDC}$ (1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid, $1.5~\mathrm{\mmol}\cdot$ ) and a mixture of ${\mathrm{FeCl}}_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $(0.5~\mathrm{\mmol})$ , ${\mathrm{CoCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.5\ \mathrm{mmol})$ , and ${\mathrm{NiCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.5\ \mathrm{mmol})$ ) were mixed in a solvent solution composed of DMF $(35~\mathrm{mL})$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (2.5 mL), and ethanol $2.5\mathrm{mL}$ ) under stirring for $10\mathrm{min}$ , followed by addition of a modulator, HF (48 wt. $\%$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $0.15~\mathrm{mL}$ ). The above solution was transferred into a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflonlined autoclave with one pre-treated NF (2.7 cm x 2.7 cm in size) placed inside at an inclined angle, for solvothermal reactions at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{~h~}$ . The autoclave was let cool to room temperature for product collection. The MOF loaded NF, termed as MOF/NF, collected at the bottom of the autoclave reactor was washed with DI water and ethanol. The MOF/NF was dried at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain the product FCN-MOF/NF electrode. When necessary for characterization purposes, such as XRD, ICP-OES, and FT-IR, the MOFs were removed from the MOF/NF composites with ultrasonication in ethanol for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ .
For comparison purposes, MOF/NF electrodes with MOFs synthesized using two (with equimolar precursors) or single metallic precursors were also fabricated. These electrodes are denoted as CNMOF/NF, FN-MOF/NF, FC-MOF/NF, F-MOF/NF, C-MOF/NF, and N-MOF/NF, with F, C, and N referring to Fe, Co, and Ni precursors, respectively used in the synthetic process of the MOF. It is to be noted that the notation of the product MOFs is based on metal salts used in the precursor solution, not the metal ions contained in them. The detailed reaction conditions are given in Table S1. The mass loading of FCN-MOF on NF was determined with ICP-OES to be $0.45\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , based on data collected from comparison of FCN-MOF/NF and FCN-MOF removed off from FCN-MOF/NF.
For fabrication of the $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}/\mathrm{NF}$ electrode, $1.8\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst powder was dispersed in $280\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $50\%$ aqueous ethanol and $120~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of polyvinylidene fluoride $(0.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in N-methylpyrrolidone as binder) under ultrasonic agitation for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ to form an ink-like suspension. One hundred $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the ink-like suspension was drop cast onto NF, followed by drying at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , to afford the $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}/\mathrm{NF}$ electrode with a catalyst mass loading of $0.45\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
## 2.3. Electrochemical Measurements
The electrocatalytic performances of the MOF/NF composite electrodes towards the OER were investigated on a CHI6275D electrochemical workstation under a three-electrode setup in $1\mathbf{M}$ aqueous KOH solution, using the MOF/NF composite electrode as the working electrode, platinum plate $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\cdot~}\mathrm{~\boldmath~\times~}1$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) as the counter electrode, and $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ as the reference electrode. Before recording the electrochemical responses, the working electrode was cycled 30 times with cyclic voltammetry at a scan rate of $100\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ to condition the electrode for stable current densities. After the conditioning, all linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) polarization curves of the electrodes were recorded at a scan rate of $1\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ unless otherwise noted. The potential values for the OER in this study were converted and referred to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) using the Nernst equation, $\mathrm{E_{RHE}=E_{H g/H g O}+0.118+0.059\times p H,}$ where $\mathrm{E_{Hg/HgO}}$ is the experimentally measured potential against the $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ reference electrode. The overpotential $(\upeta)$ was calculated by using the equation: $\upeta=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{RHE}}-1.23$ for the OER at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . All LSV polarization curves were iR-corrected with respect to the involved solution resistances. The reported current densities were based on the geometric area of NF. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted in the frequency range of $100\mathrm{kHz}$ to $10~\mathrm{{mHz}}$ , with the MOF/NF composite electrode as the working electrode operated at a suitable potential, at which the working electrode undergoes the OER. The measured EIS data were fitted to a suitable equivalent circuit model to extract the key kinetics parameter, the charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ .
For turnover frequency (TOF) calculations, a reduction peak method was used to estimate the number of surface active sites.[61] In detail, the reduction peak area of the electrode is measured first, and the area is further divided by the scan rate $\mathrm{100~mV~s^{-1}},$ ) to give the total charge involved in the reduction process. The number of electrons involved in the reduction process is calculated by dividing the charge by the unit charge of an electron $\left(1.602\mathrm{~x~}10^{-19}\mathrm{C}\right)$ . Here, the reduction process is considered as a single electron transfer reaction, and the number of electrons obtained above thus is exactly the same with the number of surface active sites $(\varGamma)$ . From the calculated value of $T_{\mathbf{\delta}}$ , the TOF can be determined from the following equation,
$$
\mathrm{TOF}=j\mathrm{~x~}N_{A}/n\mathrm{~x~}F\mathrm{~x~}T.
$$
Here, j stands for the current density $\mathrm{(A~cm^{-2}}\cdot$ ) achieved at a specific potential (here, 1.48 V (vs. RHE)), $N_{A}$ is the Avogadro number, $n$ is the number of electron transfer involved in the OER (4), and $F$ is the Faraday constant (96485 C).
## 2.4. In situ Raman Experiments
A specially made Teflon based electrochemical cell was used for the combined electrochemical and in situ Raman spectroscopy study. This cell contains a Pt wire and an $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ (CH instruments) electrode, serving as the counter and reference electrodes, respectively. The MOF/NF electrode was used as the working electrode. In situ Raman spectra of the sample electrodes were recorded using a confocal Raman microscope (Horiba HR800 UV). The laser beam ( $532\ \mathrm{nm}$ , green) was focused through a microscope objective $(\times50)$ onto a circular area of $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in diameter on the MOF/NF electrode and the backscattered light was collected through the same objective. The in situ Raman experiments were conducted on the FCN-MOF/NF, CN-MOF/NF, and N-MOF/NF electrodes at increasing OER potentials (no potential, at overpotentials $\mathbf{\tau}=0$ , 50, 100, 150, 200, 250, $300~\mathrm{mV}$ , with no potential after in-situ measurement, and then again in-situ measurement at overpotential $\mathrm{\Omega}=300\ \mathrm{mV},$ ) in 1 M KOH by using green $532\mathrm{nm},$ ) excitation laser lines.
