## 3. Results and Discussion
The trimetallic MOF was grown in situ on the skeleton surfaces of NF with a one-step solvothermal process ( $\mathrm{150~^{\circ}C/15~h}^{\circ}$ ), using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ as the organic linker and $\mathrm{FeCl_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O,C o C l_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}.$ and ${\mathrm{NiCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the Fe, Co, and Ni sources, respectively (Scheme 1). The molar ratio of the metallic precursors, Fe:Co:Ni, was controlled at 1:1:1 for fabrication of the FCN-MOF/NF electrode. For comparison purposes, MOF/NF electrodes with MOFs synthesized using two (with equimolar precursors) or single metallic precursors were also fabricated. These electrodes are denoted as CN-MOF/NF, FNMOF/NF, FC-MOF/NF, F-MOF/NF, C-MOF/NF, and N-MOF/NF, with F, C, and N referring to Fe, Co, and Ni precursors, respectively used in the synthetic process of the MOF (The detailed formulation and reaction conditions are given in Table S1). Figures 1a-1h show the scanning electron microscope (SEM) images of FCN-MOF/NF, CN-MOF/NF, FN-MOF/NF, and FC-MOF/NF at low and high resolutions. The SEM images of low resolutions reveal that all four MOFs were grown uniformly with full coverage on the skeleton surfaces of NF (Figures 1a, 1c, 1e, and 1g).  And as evident from the corresponding highresolution SEM images, all four MOFs are with a nanoslab morphology, only that FCN-MOF (Figure 1b) is significantly smaller and thinner than CN-MOF (Figure 1d), FN-MOF (Figure 1f), and FC-MOF (Figure 1h). The small and thin structure of FCN-MOF is beneficial for electrocatalysis because of the associated high exposed surface area and high percentage of exposed active sites of the catalyst.
Next, the crystalline structure of the four MOFs was examined with the powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis of the MOFs removed from the corresponding MOF/NF electrodes. From Figure S1, it is observed that the powder XRD patterns of all four MOFs match well with each other, indicating that they are with the same MOF structure. Further comparison of the powder XRD patterns of the four MOFs with the simulated powder XRD pattern of $[(\mathrm{Co_{1-x}F e_{x}})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})]$ -MOF [60] shows that all four MOFs are isostructural with that of $[(\mathrm{Co_{1-x}F e_{x}})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})]$ -MOF. Figure S2 shows the Fouriertransform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of the four MOF samples. Common absorption peaks were observed at around 1585, 1500, 1365, and $750~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which can be ascribed to the vibrations of the aromatic carboxylate groups [69]. The peaks identified at around 1585 and $1365~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belong to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of the carboxylate group, respectively, whereas the one at around $1500\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is contributed by the stretching vibrations of aromatic C-H bonds. The peak located at around $3600\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is from the O-H stretching vibrations. The peak at $750\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to bending vibrations of aromatic $\mathrm{C-H}$ . The two peaks at around 520 and $560\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to $(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}_{\mathrm{linker}}),$ ) vibrations [70], and the peak around $830\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to $\mathbf{M}(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-OH})\mathbf{M}$ group present in all four MOFs [71]. The FTIR spectra of the four MOFs match well with each other and with those reported in relevant literature [60, 67, 69], further confirming that all four MOFs possess the same crystalline structure and are isostructural with previously reported FeCo-MOF [60], Co-MOF [69], and Ni-MOF [67].
The transmission electron microscopy energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (TEM-EDX) (Figure 2a) and SEM-EDX (Figure S3) elemental mapping of FCN-MOF confirms the presence of all constituent elements, Fe, Co, Ni, C, and O, and the uniform distributions of these elements over the catalyst. In particular, the TEM-EDX elemental mapping images, because of the high spatial resolution of the TEM-EDX, well retain the shape of the sample. The inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) for the four MOFs were performed to further confirm the metallic composition of the MOFs (Table 1). The results indicate that the atomic ratio of Fe:Co:Ni is around 1:1:1 in FCNMOF, which is in good agreement with the metallic compositions estimated from TEM-EDX (Figure 2b) and SEM-EDX (Figure S3). Hence the molecular composition of FCN-MOF is determined to be $[\mathrm{FeCoNi(OH)_{3}(B D C)_{1.5}}]$ based on the information obtained from powder XRD, FT-IR, EDX, and ICPOES. As to the MOFs prepared from using two metallic precursors, CN-MOF (Table 1 & Figure S4) and FN-MOF (Table 1 & Figure S5), have an atomic ratio of 1:2 for Co:Ni and 1:1.5 for Fe:Ni, respectively as concluded from the corresponding ICP-OES and SEM-EDX results. Interestingly, for FC-MOF, both ICP-OES (Table 1) and SEM-EDX (Figure S6) results reveal that FC-MOF contains not only Fe and Co from the metal salt precursors but also Ni from nickel foam. Evidently, the modulator, HF, of the solvothermal process dissolves a small amount of NF to produce $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ to participate in the MOF formation reaction [48] to give the presence of Ni in FC-MOF, making FC-MOF a trimetallic MOF instead of bimetallic MOF. FC-MOF contains all three metallic components in an atomic ratio Fe:Co:Ni of around 1:0.5:0.33. Since all four MOFs are isostructural with each other as well as with previously reported FeCo-MOF [60], Co-MOF [69], and Ni-MOF [67], the general molecular structure of the product MOF can be proposed and is given in Figure S7 for better understanding. Detailed description of the molecular structure is offered in the caption of Figure S7.
Based on the configurational entropy concept proposed in this work, the perfectly composition-balanced product, possessing equimolar active species, would exhibit the maximum extent of randomness in elemental distribution and thus the most pronounced synergistic effects between the participating active species.
To further study the effects of precursor molar ratios on product morphology, composition, and OER performances, three comparison electrodes were fabricated at precursor metallic molar ratios Fe:Co:Ni of 1:1:1 (same as the one used to prepare FCN-MOF/NF), 3:1:1, and 1:3:1, with  the obtained products named as FCN(1:1:1)-MOF/NF, FCN(3:1:1)-MOF/NF, and FCN(1:3:1)-MOF/NF, respectively. The detailed reaction formulations and conditions are summarized in Table S2. It is to be noted that the case of precursor Fe:Co:Ni of 1:1:3 is not as relevant as the above three cases because of the involvement of Ni dissolution from NF during the solvothermal process, making the true precursor molar ratio of Ni higher than 3. Therefore, the case of 1:1:3 was not included for comparison. The product morphology, composition, and OER performances of FCN(1:1:1)-MOF/NF, FCN(3:1:1)-MOF/NF, and FCN(1:3:1)- MOF/NF were characterized with SEM, SEM-EDX, and LSV (Figure S8). Evidently, all three products show similar morphology of nanoslabs of similar dimensions, possess the same equimolar compositions, and exhibit almost overlapping LSV polarization curves. The result of the same equimolar product compositions from three different starting precursor compositions implies that the present synthetic process is self-modulated regardless of the starting precursor composition.
The effect of electrolyte concentrations toward the electrocatalytic efficiency of the bestperforming electrode, FCN-MOF/NF, was also examined. The electrode was tested in 0.1 M KOH, a molar concentration only one tenth of 1 M KOH, and the resulting LSV curve is presented in Figure S9. The electrolytic efficiency is not as good as that measured in 1 M KOH as expected, but remains decent with an η10 of $235\mathrm{mV}$ .
Furthermore, the kinetics of the OER were assessed by Tafel slope derived from the corresponding LSV polarization curves. As observed in Figure 3c, the FCN-MOF/NF electrode exhibits the smallest Tafel slope of $29.5\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , which is lower than those of the CN-MOF/NF $(49.8\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ ), FN-MOF/NF $\mathrm{41.7~mV~dec^{-1}},$ ), FC-MOF/NF $31.4~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1},$ ), $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}/\mathrm{NF}$ $\mathrm{53.2~mV~dec^{-1}}$ ), and blank NF $(55.4\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ ) electrodes. This clearly indicates that the FCN-MOF/NF electrode holds the fastest OER kinetics over the other tested electrodes as well as most of recently reported top-notch NF based composite OER electrocatalysts in 1 M KOH, including CoFeZr oxides/NF ${\mathsf{S}}4.2{\mathrm{~mV~dec}}^{-1},$ ) [79], Ni2PVP2/NF (47 mV dec-1) [80], CoFe@NiFe/NF (45.7 mV dec-1) [81], FeOOH/NPC-NF ( $33.8~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1) [82], (Ni-Fe)Sx/NiFe(OH)y-NF $(58\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ ) [83], and NC/CuCo/CuCoOx-NF $(88\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ ) [84].
The OER kinetics were further investigated with electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements. The EIS was conducted at $1.60\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE), at which all tested electrodes underwent the OER process, and in the frequency range of $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ to $10~\mathrm{{mHz}}$ in 1 M KOH. The charge transfer resistances $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ , the key parameter to quantify the kinetic behavior of the catalyst, were obtained by fitting the Nyquist plots (Figure 3d) with an equivalent circuit model (Figure S10). Accordingly, the smallest $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of $0.23\Omega$ was obtained for the FCN-MOF/NF electrode, which is significantly smaller than those of the FC-MOF/NF $\left(0.47~\Omega\right)$ and FN-MOF/NF $(0.75\Omega)$ electrodes, and substantially lower than those of the CN-MOF/NF $\left(1.60\Omega\right)$ and blank NF $(4.23~\Omega)$ electrodes, consistent with the trend observed in overpotentials and Tafel slopes. Moreover, electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) is an important parameter for quantification of the OER efficiencies. In general, catalytic efficiencies are determined considering both the quantity and quality (intrinsic activity) of exposed active sites of the catalyst. The quantity of the exposed active sites can be estimated with ECSA, which in turn is proportional to the double layer capacitances $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ of the catalyst. In order to estimate ECSA for the sample electrodes, cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves at increasing scan rates were recorded within a nonFaradaic OER potential window (0.7-0.8 V vs. RHE), Figure S11 for FCN-MOF/NF, Figure S12 for CNMOF/NF, Figure S13 for FN-MOF/NF, Figure S14 for FC-MOF/NF, and Figure S15 for blank NF. The value of $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ was then calculated by using the slope (slope $=2C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ ) of the current density difference $(\Delta j)$ at $0.75\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE) against scan rate plot (Figure 3e). From the calculated $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value, the corresponding ECSA can be estimated by using a reference specific capacitance $(C_{s})$ $\mathrm{{\underline{{{\cal{E}}}}C S A}}=C_{d l}/C_{s})$ . Here, a commonly adopted $C_{s}$ of $0.040~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ is used [85]. The ECSA thus obtained are 52.50, 35.50 25.13, 12.50, and $7.13\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for FCN-MOF/NF, FC-MOF/NF, FN-MOF/NF, CN-MOF/NF, and blank NF respectively, indicating that FCN-MOF/NF possesses a maximum amount of exposed active sites among the five samples. Furthermore, the ECSA-normalized LSV curves are prepared and presented in Figure S16. Evidently, the trend remains the same as that observed in the non-ECSA-normalized LSV plot, Fig. 3a. As to the quality, the intrinsic activity, of the exposed active sites, it can be quantified with turnover frequencies (TOFs). Here, we adopt a reduction peak area method proposed in literature to determine the key parameter, the number of surface active sites, for determination of TOFs [61]. TOFs were calculated at $1.48{\mathrm{~V}}$ (vs. RHE) and the results are summarized in Figure 3f. The FCN-MOF/NF electrode exhibits the highest TOF value of $0.865\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ , with the FC-MOF/NF electrode (a trimetallic MOF based electrode) coming next at $0.733\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . This implies the fundamental advantage of synergistic effects arising from ternary over binary metallic MOFs, particularly the composition-balanced one being with the most pronounced synergistic effects. Taking both ECSA and TOF into consideration gives the OER efficiency trend, in excellent agreement with that of overpotentials.
On the whole, comparison of the OER performances of all four MOFs clearly shows that trimetallic MOFs (FCN-MOF and FC-MOF) are more efficient electrocatalysts than bimetallic MOFs (FN-MOF and CN-MOF). Between the two trimetallic MOFs, the one with the product Fe:Co:Ni ratio of 1:1:1 (FCN-MOF) exhibits significantly better performances than those of the one with the product Fe:Co:Ni ratio of 1:0.5:0.33 (FC-MOF). One may infer that MOFs with more balanced metallic compositions, (1:1:1) for FCN-MOF as one example, give stronger synergistic effects than those with less balanced metallic components, (1:0.5:0.33) for FC-MOF as one example, as suggested by the proposed maximized-entropy concept. From Eq. (1), the configurational entropy can be derived to reach the maximum of RlnN when the system is equimolar in active species (all $x_{\mathrm{i}}$ being equal to 1/N). This maximum increases with increasing $N$ , for example, $0.693R$ for binary systems and $1.099R$ for ternary systems. Consequently, for Fe, Co, and Ni based trimetallic MOFs, the composition-balanced FCN-MOF exhibits better OER efficiencies than the less composition-balanced FC-MOF. Furthermore, composition-balanced trimetallic MOFs are expected to perform better than corresponding bimetallic MOFs, for example, FCN-MOF outperforming FN-MOF and CN-MOF in OER efficiencies. In the meantime, on comparing the OER performances of the two bimetallic MOFs, FN-MOF and CN-MOF, one can further conclude that Fe incorporation in Ni-MOF induces stronger intra-molecular synergetic effects over Co incorporation in Ni-MOF for the OER.
The first two are Fe, Co, and Ni based trimetallic MOF/NF electrodes, with FCN-MOF/NF being perfectly composition-balanced. The next two are Fe and Ni based bimetallic MOF/NF electrodes, with FN-MOF/NF being the more composition-balanced one. The next two further down in the list are Co and Ni based bimetallic MOF/NF electrodes, with CN-MOF/NF being the more composition-balanced one. The last one is a monometallic MOF/NF electrode, without the presence of heterometallic synergistic effects for OER enhancements. It is evident that the OER efficiency follows the maximized-entropy concept and Fe incorporation is synergistically more efficient than Co incorporation toward Ni-MOF in terms of OER enhancements.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted for the four best performing electrodes, namely, FCN-MOF/NF, FC-MOF/NF, FN-MOF/NF, and F-MOF/NF to investigate the origin of the synergistic effects (Figure 4). The full survey spectra of the four electrodes (Figure 4a) show that FCNMOF and FC-MOF are trimetallic MOFs, and FN-MOF and F-MOF are bimetallic MOFs as we have seen in the EDX results. In addition, the high-resolution (HR) XPS spectra of Fe2p (Figure 4b) of all four electrodes exhibit binding energy peaks at around $710.5~\mathrm{eV}$ and $724{\mathrm{~eV}}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, indicating the $^{2+}$ oxidation state of iron [48]. The HR-XPS spectra of Co2p of FCNMOF/NF and FC-MOF/NF (Figure $\mathrm{4c}$ ) display the binding energy peaks at around 780 and $796\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Co2p1/2}$ , respectively, supporting the $^{2+}$ oxidation state of cobalt in FCN-MOF and FC-MOF [51]. In the case of Ni2p (Figure 4d), the binding energy peaks for $\mathrm{Ni}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ are observed at around 855 and 873 eV, respectively, indicating the $^{2+}$ oxidation state of $\mathrm{Ni}$ in all four electrodes [58]. It is interesting to note that the characteristic peaks of Fe2p, Co2p, and Ni2p shift to higher binding energies for the MOF/NF electrodes, possessing more constituent metals and more balanced compositions. Evidently, the detail metallic composition of the MOF modifies local electronic structure and thus electrocatalytic activities of the metal centers. Higher binding energies imply more elevated oxidation states and thus higher oxidative potentials to catalyze the OER. Compared with the bimetallic MOF/NF electrodes, the trimetallic MOF/NF electrodes give more pronounced up-shifts in binding energies. This may originate from the change in the coordination environment of the metal centers caused by introduction of the third metal, which is crucial in modulating electronic properties of the metal centers and thus promoting catalyzation of the OER [37]. Between the two trimetallic MOF/NF electrodes, the one having more balanced composition (FCN-MOF/NF) shifts slightly more than that of the less balanced FC-MOF/NF, in consistent with the maximized entropy concept proposed in this study.
The maximized-entropy concept proposed here for maximized synergistic enhancements achieved by composition-balanced multi-component catalysts works not only for the present MOF based electrocatalysts, but also for other metal based inorganic catalysts. It has been observed that compositionbalanced bi- and tri-metallic component based electrocatalysts, having equimolar metallic components (i.e., with an atomic ratio of $1:1$ or $1:1:1$ ), show better electrocatalytic performances [40, 86-91]. It has also been proved both experimentally and theoretically (with density functional theory (DFT) calculations) that equimolar mixed metals based electrocatalysts show the highest electrocatalytic efficiency over the same mixed metals based electrocatalysts containing less composition-balanced metallic components [40, 89-91]. For instance, Kibsgaard et al. reported that the metallic compositionbalanced mixed metal phosphides, Fe0.5Co0.5P, possessed near-optimal hydrogen adsorption free energy $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}})$ and exhibited the highest HER efficiency among all mixed metal phosphide catalysts they investigated [91]. In another case, DFT simulations suggested that the addition of Fe can drastically reduce the hydrogen adsorption free energy of $\mathrm{Co2P}$ . And with a Co:Fe ratio close to 1:1, the hydrogen adsorption free energy of the mixed metal phosphides, $(\mathbf{C_{00.5}F e}_{0.5})_{2}\mathbf{P}$ , reached a value of 0.0477 eV, which is comparable to 0.09 eV of Pt, leading to the highest HER efficiency among all samples the authors tested [89]. Yu et al. reported that ternary metal phosphides exhibited versatile electrocatalytic activities that were strongly dependent on the Ni:Co ratios and $\mathrm{Ni_{0.51}C o_{0.49}P}$ films were found to have the highest electrocatalytic activities for both HER and OER [90].
