It has been proved with DFT simulations and X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) analyses that the coordinatively unsaturated metal sites of MOFs improves the catalytic activity of the OER catalysts [50]. In addition, it was proposed that partial electron transfer occurs between different metal ions through the bridging oxygen of the organic ligand, and the charge transfer process was well explained in terms of relevant electronic structures [50]. It has also been reported that such coupling between metal ions could lead to better OER performances [50]. The importance of the coupling between different metal ions for enhancing the OER activity is experimentally confirmed here with systematic studies of MOF based electrocatalysts with relevant metallic composition design, from which trimetallic MOFs exhibit significantly better electrocatalytic efficiencies than bimetallic and monometallic MOFs. Hence, manipulation of the interactions between neighboring metal ions in MOFs, containing coordinatively unsaturated metal sites, proves to be an efficient route to develop high-performance OER electrocatalyts. Most importantly, the maximized-entropy concept proposed here provides useful guidelines for composition design of multi-component catalysts.
In addition to electrocatalytic efficiencies, long-term durability of the electrocatalyst at large current densities is also vital for its large-scale applications. The durability of FCN-MOF/NF was tested against the working current densities of 100 and $1,000\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ under chronoamperometric $(i\ –t)$ mode for $50\mathrm{h}$ . The FCN-MOF/NF electrode did not show appreciable decay in current densities when operated at $100\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ and experienced only $5\%$ decay in current densities when operated at the industrially relevant high current density of $1,000\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ after $50\mathrm{~h~}$ continuous operation (Figure 6a). The outstanding durability of FCN-MOF/NF can be further confirmed with SEM, powder XRD, HR-XPS, and CVs before and after the stability test. It is noteworthy to mention that the CV curves recorded before and after the OER stability test (Figure 6b) almost overlap with each other, suggesting the outstanding electrochemical and mechanical robustness of the FCN-MOF/NF electrode. Furthermore, the thin nanoslab structure of FCN-MOF is well-maintained after the stability test as can be seen from the inset of Figure 6b and not much changes in crystalline structure occur as can be judged from the similar XRD patterns of FCN-MOF before and after the durability test (Figure 6c). The outstanding stability of FCNMOF/NF is attributed in part to the in-situ growth of FCN-MOF on the skeleton surfaces of the nickel foam. The binder-free and in-situ growth strategy not only improve the mechanical adhesion and contact between the MOF and NF, but also enable strong synergistic effects arising from the interactions among the uniformly well-dispersed and well-mixed Fe, Co, and Ni ions in FCN-MOF for much enhanced intrinsic activities towards the OER. Figure S27 displays the XPS full survey spectra of FCN-MOF/NF before and after the stability test and the HR-XPS spectra of Fe2p, Co2p, and Ni2p after the stability test. It is to be noted that the XPS spectra recorded before and after the stability test match well with each other, and the HR-XPS spectra of Fe2p, Co2p, and Ni2p collected after the stability test mostly remains to those before the stability test. In conclusion, the stability of FCN-MOF/NF is outstanding even tested at an ultrahigh current density of $1,000\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . Furthermore, the amount of oxygen produced from the OER was measured with gas chromatography by taking FCN-MOF/NF as the working electrode for operation at a current density of $500\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ for $75~\mathrm{{min}}$ . It is quite apparent from Figure 6d that the theoretical and experimental results of oxygen production are in excellent agreement, indicating close to $100\%$ Faradaic efficiencies for the OER and absence of side reactions.
Finally, the OER performances of FCN-MOF/NF are compared with recently reported top ranked metal based inorganic (Table S6) and MOF based (Table S7) OER electrocatalysts (in 1 M KOH) with respect to key electrocatalytic performance parameters, including η at specific current densities, Tafel slope, and durability. Evidently, FCN-MOF/NF outperforms almost all top rated OER electrocatalysts in overpotentials at low $\left(\upeta_{10}\right)$ as well as high current densities (η500 and η1000) and Tafel slope (Table S6 and
S7). Moreover, except for a very few previous works [22, 24, 32, 35] including ours [57-59], the stability was evaluated only at low current densities (less than $100\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}$ ), which is not high enough to assess their potential industrial applicability. In that point of view, the present FCN-MOF/NF, which possesses outstanding stability even at the ultrahigh current density of $1,000\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , stands out.
## 4. Conclusions
Ultra-efficient OER electrocatalysts were developed based on the maximized-entropy concept for maximum synergistic enhancements in electrocatalytic performances. Accordingly, compositionbalanced Fe, Co, and Ni based trimetallic MOFs, [FeCoNi(OH)3(BDC)1.5], were grown in-situ on skeleton surfaces of a nickel foam, FCN-MOF/NF, as ultra-efficient and stable non-precious metal based electrocatalysts for the OER under alkaline conditions. The product catalyst, with an equimolar metallic composition, possesses maximum synergistic effects towards the OER, exhibiting outstanding OER performances with ultralow overpotentials of 196 and $284\mathrm{mV}$ at current densities of 10 and $1{,}000\ \mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, and an ultralow Tafel slope of $29.5\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ . At a commercially relevant high current density of $1,000\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ , the OER performances of FCN-MOF/NF were well maintained after a 50- hour continuous operation. Exploration and design of the synergistic effects between constituent active species of multi-component catalysts are an effective way to drastically boost the electrocatalytic performances of the catalysts. The maximized-entropy concept proposed here provides useful guidelines for composition design of multi-component catalysts to maximize synergistic effects for electrocatalytic enhancements.
